## DB Bloat Concern — Addressed

> [!NOTE]
> **Transient = 1 single row per day. Zero bloat.**
>
> The daily counter uses ONE WordPress transient: `ofast_email_sent_2026-06-04`. That's literally 1 row in `wp_options`. When it expires at midnight, WordPress auto-deletes it on the next page load (no cron needed for cleanup — WP does this in `wp_loaded`). 
>
> Yesterday's transient is gone before today's first send. At any moment, there is exactly **1 row** in the database for the counter. Compare this to alternatives:
> - Custom table approach: requires CREATE TABLE, migration, manual cleanup cron
> - Option-based approach: persists forever, needs manual reset logic
> - Transient: self-cleaning, zero maintenance, exactly the right tool

**For campaign quota enforcement:** When a free campaign exhausts its daily quota mid-send, we simply update the existing campaign row's `next_run` to tomorrow. No new rows, no new tables — just one `UPDATE` on a row that already exists.

