## Pros, Cons & Senior Dev Assessment

### Pros (Why This Architecture is Sound)

| Aspect | Assessment |
|--------|-----------|
| **Separation of concerns** | Quota only touches Ofast Emailer — zero interference with WP core, WooCommerce, or any other plugin's email pipeline |
| **Minimal DB footprint** | 1 transient row/day (auto-cleaned). Retry table grows slowly and has 7-day cleanup |
| **Scalable** | Adding tiers later = change one constant. Filter hook (`ofast_email_free_daily_limit`) lets hosts/agencies override per-site |
| **Graceful degradation** | Campaigns auto-resume next day, no data loss, no manual intervention needed |
| **No performance impact** | Zero hooks on the hot `wp_mail()` path for counting — only called inside our own code |
| **Battle-tested pattern** | Transients for daily counters is used by Wordfence, Jetpack, WPForms — proven at scale |
| **Maintainable** | 2 new self-contained classes, follows existing patterns (compare to how fallback SMTP was added) |

### Cons & Mitigations

| Risk | Severity | Mitigation |
|------|----------|------------|
| User could bypass by calling `wp_mail()` directly | Low | That's fine — we gate the UI, not all email. Same pattern as Mailchimp/ConvertKit |
| Retry cron (5 min) on shared hosting | Low | WP already runs cron on page loads. 5-min is standard. Retries process max 20 emails per run — lightweight |
| Retry table needs cleanup | Low | 7-day auto-cleanup cron (same pattern as existing `ofast_email_cleanup` and `ofast_smtp_cleanup_logs`) |
| Object cache could interfere with transients | Very Low | Sites with Redis/Memcached handle transients even better (in-memory, never hits DB) |

### Is This a Dead Trap? No.

**This is the exact same pattern used by:**
- **WPForms**: Free = 20 notifications/form, Pro = unlimited
- **FluentSMTP**: Free email logging, Pro = advanced features
- **MailPoet**: Free = 1,000 subscribers, Pro = unlimited

The architecture is **additive** (2 new files, 4 small modifications). If you ever decide to remove the quota, you delete 1 class and remove ~15 lines from 2 files. The retry system is independently useful and can stay regardless.

**Future-proof:** If you add more tiers (e.g., "Business" = 500/day), you only change the `get_daily_limit()` method to check license tier. The rest of the system doesn't care.

